WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:07.320
Film Courage: E.K. should a writer share their
first, second, even third of their manuscript

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.580
with family and friends?

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.580
E.K.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.490
Prescott, Ph.D.: That is a really interesting
question.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:14.490
Yes and no.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:24.689
I shared mine with family, but not all family
are writers and can give you the kind of feedback

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:26.050
you want.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:33.090
But what I did was, I share it after it was
done because I found telling the story wasn’t

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.750
that effective, they had to see it done.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.970
Or reading a chapter here and there wasn’t
effective.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.930
They really didn’t know what I was trying
to do.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:45.750
Unless you want somebody to edit it, now that’s
different.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:51.410
But if you want feedback on the story, they
couldn’t get it into their…they are not

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:57.281
educators (educators tend to…especially
English teachers can look at it and kind of

00:00:57.281 --> 00:00:58.560
give you some feedback).

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.140
So when it was done, yes I got some feedback.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.310
And everybody liked it but…”Yeah, it’s
good?

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.590
But will it sell?

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:11.789
Will it get a publisher?”

00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:13.030
And things like that.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:17.619
But not much more than that, I just did a
little.

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:22.619
So to recommend it, you’re not going to
get what you want but you’re going to get

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:27.990
enough to give you some hope or ideas of what
you need to do.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.810
Film Courage: So were the publishers that
you submitted it to the first ones to actually

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.619
see the finished product?

00:01:35.619 --> 00:01:39.509
Or did you get it to any person who was sort
of a confidante that you trusted?

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:40.509
E.K.

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:44.670
Prescott: No, I didn’t have anybody look
at it except my husband.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.259
But that was about it.

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:51.590
But my publisher read the whole book.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:58.720
She said “When it’s ready, send it to
me.”

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.560
And she called and said “I can’t put it
down!

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.630
I had to change what I was doing over the
weekend.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.170
It’s really good.”

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.570
I said “I told you it was good.”

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.250
She said “No, no, no…everybody says there
book is good.This is good!

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.680
How did you write such as good book?”

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.220
And I said “Well, I don’t know?

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:16.390
I just wrote it.”

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:21.560
So she believed in me and she met me and she
knew I’m serious.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.560
I’m a serious writer.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.880
I’m not just writing one book, I’m writing
six.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.450
And so we went from there and I like her.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:32.080
We had a good chemistry and went from there.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.340
And I liked her, too because she was a one-stop-shop.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:42.200
She’s at Five Star Publishing and her name
is Linda and she’s one-stop-shop and nothing

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.290
goes without my approval.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.930
But they have quality work…I mean…quality
work.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:51.660
Film Courage: How would you give advice to
a first time writer on getting feedback on

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.120
a first, second or third draft of something?

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.510
What would you say, listen to your gut?

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:04.680
Because there is that perception sometimes
when you are showing something to someone

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:05.680
you know.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.840
Like my grandmother used to say “No man
is a prophet in his own land.”

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.300
So sometimes people look at things differently
when they know you?

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.300
E.K.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.540
Prescott: Or they are afraid to say too much
or be critical.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:22.730
And they tend to think they need to tell you
about editing instead of revisions.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:28.420
And see the common person might think I need
to look at the periods, and the dots and the

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.970
sentences are wrong and that’s not what
you want.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:32.500
You want “Did you like the characters?”

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:33.500
Did you like this?

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:34.500
Did you like that?

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.850
Because you can hire an editor to fix all
that stuff.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:46.350
So I think that answer (from my perspective)
came to me as an after fact (after the book

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.830
was out).

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:53.710
We gave some advance copies out to some friends
and we sent some friends some regular copies.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:58.760
And it was really interesting and from an
English teacher’s perspective of what they

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.290
said.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:05.280
Not one person out of the ten people I targeted
to send it to…I said “Tell me what you

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.560
liked.”

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.480
I didn’t know what they didn’t like at
first.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.260
And not one person said the same thing.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:19.350
Now in answering your question, I wouldn’t
get the same from every person and originally

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.690
I might think that was wrong.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:28.560
But as an English teacher teaching literature
in high school, a good piece of literature

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.750
has different perspectives.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.940
And that is a mark of good literature.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:39.600
So when I got something different from each
person, then I (from my point of view) had

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.160
a good piece of writing.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.160
Because one person said “I love Wiki!”

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.340
Or “I love Maisie.”

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:52.070
Or “I love the history” Or…I had an
accountant say “I like the way you write.”

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:57.690
So you have a variety of things and if you’re
getting that variety, you have a decent piece

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:58.100
of work.

